#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1260.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065114} :activity/id #uuid "8829b364-1582-5e1a-894c-d8837d69b705" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050929 :topic/id #uuid "7280f087-059d-5524-8f8b-a1fe79b4ef9d" :topic/math-academy-id 317} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065114 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065444 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we introduce a method of integration that can be used in cases where integration by substitution fails. The method is known as **integration by parts**. The so-called **by-parts formula** is \n\n$$\n\\int u\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x= uv- \\int v\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\n\\int uv'\\,\\textrm{d}x= uv- \\int vu'\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis method is handy for integrating products of functions where one factor can be easily integrated, and the other factor can easily be differentiated. \n\nLet's see how to apply it to evaluate the integral \n\n$$\n\\int xe^{2x} \\textrm{d}x\\,.\n$$\n\nThe idea is to call one of the factors $u,$ the other factor $v',$ and then apply the by-parts formula. We'll choose the following:\n\n$$\n\\int \\underbrace{\\color{blue}x}_{\\large\\color{blue}u}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{red}e^{2x}}_{\\large\\color{red}v'} \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nNow, if ${\\color{blue}u} = {\\color{blue}x}$ then we differentiate to get ${\\color{blue}u'} = {\\color{blue}1}.$ And if ${\\color{red}v'} = {\\color{red}e^{2x}}$ then we integrate to get ${\\color{red}v} = {\\color{red}\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{2x}}.$ Note that we don't bother with the constant of integration here.\n\nWe now have everything we need, so we can substitute into the \"by-parts\" formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {{x\\cdot {e}^{2x}}_{⏟}}_{u{v}^{′}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={{x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}}_{⏟}}_{uv}-\\int {{\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}\\cdot 1}_{⏟}}_{v{u}^{′}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}(2x-1)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done! Notice that integration by parts allows us to take a complicated integral $\\displaystyle \\int xe^{2x}\\,\\textrm{d}x$ and replace it with the simpler $\\displaystyle \\int e^{2x}\\,\\textrm{d}x$ plus some additional terms." :tutorial/id #uuid "7ef18e8c-9fd4-5e14-84ee-a8fe309425c8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 892 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "66772855-684d-5b86-b797-dd142c5cce75" :step/math-academy-id 1725 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065115}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065115 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064245 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064099 :question/id #uuid "60a223c7-3d1c-50d6-be7c-330ba039635c" :question/math-academy-id "e-491"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abe88143-989a-5967-bb08-16804e31d948" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050101}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362996}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Indefinite Integral Containing an Exponential Expression"} :step/id #uuid "69d17961-eefe-570a-b3a8-ada39be9dbe8" :step/math-academy-id 1726 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065116}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065116 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064246 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064100 :question/id #uuid "804f45cd-2082-5521-b4db-a32169716626" :question/math-academy-id "e-492"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc6023d1-1461-50d1-b939-7e08f2878df4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050489}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367603}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Indefinite Integral Containing a Trigonometric Expression"} :step/id #uuid "7e9ca4a9-f3b8-546c-b51a-bb8c5db61861" :step/math-academy-id 1730 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065160}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065117 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065445 :tutorial/content "If you thought the by-parts formula looked similar to the product rule for differentiation, then you'd be right. In fact, that's precisely where it comes from!\n\nTo work out the formula, we recall the product rule for differentiation,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(uv)=u\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}+ v\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x},\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ and $v$ are functions of $x.$ \n\nIf we rearrange this formula a bit, we get\n\n$$\nu\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(uv)- v\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's now integrate both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\int u\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\int\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(uv)\\,\\textrm{d}x- \\int v\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nUsing the fundamental theorem of calculus, we can use the fact that $\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(uv)\\,\\textrm{d}x=uv,$ and we arrive at the by-parts formula:\n\n$$\n\\int u\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x= uv- \\int v\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9f435fc9-cb87-577b-89e1-cda2ce506387" :tutorial/math-academy-id 895 :tutorial/title "Deriving the By Parts Formula From the Product Rule"} :step/id #uuid "45eefd0d-11c7-5c51-857d-c39e7a530c49" :step/math-academy-id 1733 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065118}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065118 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065446 :tutorial/content "Let's go back to our first example,\n\n$$\n\\int xe^{2x} \\textrm{d}x\\,.\n$$\n\nNote that when we selected our $u$ and $v',$ we could have picked them the other way around. So instead, we could have said\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{2x}\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the by-parts formula using this selection, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{2x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}-\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, while the above is a mathematically correct statement, the integral on the right-hand side is *more* complicated than the integral we started with. Ouch!\n\nTherefore, it's crucial that we select our $u$ and $v'$ in such a way that the problem is simplified, not made more complicated." :tutorial/id #uuid "e7845bcc-bef4-5a31-8abe-39845317fbaf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 896 :tutorial/title "Final Remark"} :step/id #uuid "26fab519-16d1-54c6-997b-34fc2c75ed86" :step/math-academy-id 1734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065160 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064274 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064117 :question/id #uuid "aaeb0407-771b-5dbc-8a50-e7bc0e3477c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-6209"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3aaba0a-3b86-58c3-bbc8-22ced09bc8a8" 